|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_z_combinations_
    !! If , 
    What are
    the signatures of $1/z$, $z^2$, $-z$, $z + z$, $z/z$, $z - z$
    and $z \circ z$,
    where $z : [\lbs] \ra [\te{£}]$?
    !! is the function of Example 1?

    |> Solution

        !! The signatures 
        !! of $1/z$, $z^2$, $-z$, $z + z$ and $z/z$
        !! are respectively 
        We have

        $$
        (1/z) : [\lbs] \ra [1/\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        !! [\lbs] \ra [1/\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        $$
        $$
        z^2 : [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}^2\rt{0.1}]
        !! [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}^2\rt{0.1}]
        $$
        $$
        (-z) : [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        !! [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        $$
        $$
        (z + z) : [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        !! [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        $$
        $$
        (z/z) : [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}1\rt{0.1}]
        !! [\lbs] \ra [1]
        $$
        $$
        (z - z) : [\lbs] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        !! [\lbs] \ra [1]
        $$

        !! for $1/z$, $z^2$, $-z$, $z + z$ and $z/z$ respectively
        applying the rules of >>table_1_1 with $f = g = z$
        and of >>table_1_2 with $f = z$. 
        
        On the other hand,

        $$
        z \circ z
        $$

        is not a well-formed composition, and does not have
        a well-defined signature, since the output units of
        $z$ do not match the input units of $z$!

        !! as obtained from the rules for adding 
        !! for the function $z$ of >>table_1_1, as obtained by
        !! reciprocating 

        |> SolutionNote

            Since

            $$
            -z = (-1)z,
            $$
            $$
            z + z = 2z,
            $$

            !! and

            $$
            z - z = 0z
            $$

            three of the cases considered are just special
            cases of multiplying the function by a 
            constant. (Which does not change the signature, as long
            as the constant is dimensionless.)

            [Nb: The multiplication of a function by a constant
            is formally treated in Exercise >>_scalar_times_function_definition_#decoy:0
            of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:0.]
            !! where the multiplication of a function by a
            !! constant brings to light a “missing operation”. 
            !! we can see that three of these are just special
            !! cases of multiplying a function by a constant.

|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_f_g_f_

    What kinds of functions $f$ and $g$, signature-wise,
    does the composition 
    $$
    f \circ g \circ f
    $$
    require?

    |> Solution

        The composition requires $f$ and $g$ of the form

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB] \\
        \up{1.6}g : [\tB] \ra [\tA]
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        for some units A and B
        since the output of $f$ is forwarded to $g$, whose
        output of $g$ if forwarded back to $f$.

|> Exercise
    handle=_physicist_choose_to_write_

    If units of time and space are chosen
    such that $c = 1$ where $c$ is the speed of the light,
    why might 
    a physicist
    still choose
    to write expressions containing ‘$c$’?

    |> Solution

        !! Besides wanting to “remind the general
        !! formula”, one reason is
        !! to keep expressions dimensionally consistent;
        !! for example, if $v < c$
        !! is another speed, then

        !! $$
        !! \sqrt{1 - {v^2\over c^2}}
        !! $$

        !! is the so-called _Lorentz contraction factor_
        !! of special relativity, which is dimensionless—writing

        !! $$
        !! \sqrt{1 - v^2}
        !! $$

        !! might be numerically correct and typographically more
        !! expedient, but is dimensionally
        !! inconsistent: either a speed squared is being subtracted
        !! from a dimensionless ‘$1$’, or else we pretend
        !! that ‘$1$’ stands for ‘$c^2$’, which is also a speed squared,
        !! but then the entire square root is a speed instead of
        !! being dimensionless—failures on all sides!

        Besides wanting to “remind the general
        formula”, one reason is
        to keep expressions dimensionally consistent.
        For example, the Lorentz factor reciprocal

        $$
        \sqrt{1 - {v^2\over c^2}}
        $$

        (cf. Exercise >>_lorentz_factor_dimensionality_)
        !! is the so-called _Lorentz factor_
        !! of special relativity, which is dimensionless—writing
        would become

        $$
        \sqrt{1 - v^2}
        $$

        if 
        taking advantage of $c = 1$ to
        elide the ‘$c$’, which
        might be numerically correct and typographically more
        expedient, but is dimensionally
        inconsistent: either a speed squared is being subtracted
        from a dimensionless ‘$1$’, or, in a last-ditch effort, we pretend
        that ‘$1$’ stands for ‘$c^2$’, which is also a speed squared,
        but then the entire square root is a speed instead of
        being dimensionless, 
        is it should be—failures on all sides!
        !! as the Lorentz factor should be—failures on all sides!

|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_analysis_of_product_rule_
    Give a dimensional analysis of the (real,
    “late afternoon”) product rule (cf$.$ Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4, Exercise >>_product_rule_#decoy:16):

    |> Boxed
        $$
        \Large (fg)' = f'g + fg'
        $$

    |> Solution
        Products of the form

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        fg  \\
        f'g\up{1.9} \\
        fg'\up{1.9}
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        require $f$ and $g$ to have the same input units, but no more,
        per the multiplication rule of [Table 1.1](>>table_1_1),
        and per the fact that
        $h'$ has the same input units as $h$
        for any function $h : \rr \ra \rr$. 
        Therefore, signatures of the form

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB] \\
        g : [\tA] \ra [\tC] \up{1.6}
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        are at least necessary on either side of the equation.
        Then, evaluating further, one finds
        !! But then one finds, in order, that
        !! $$
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! fg : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC] \\
        !! \up{2.5}(fg)' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\tC\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}f' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}f'g : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\tC\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}g' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tC\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}fg' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\tC\over \tA}\right]
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        fg : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC]                    \\
        \up{1.6}(fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]     \\
        \up{1.6}f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]           \\
        \up{1.6}g' : [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA]           \\
        \up{1.6}f'g : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]       \\
        \up{1.6}fg' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]       \\
        \up{1.6}f'g + fg' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        so these signatures are also sufficient to
        evaluate either side of the equation to the end, and,
        moreover,
        $(fg)'$
        and
        $f'g + g'f$
        are found to have the same signature, concluding the 
        dimensional analysis. (= Both sides impose the same
        signature constraints, and evaluate to the same final
        signature.)

        !! so signatures as above are also _sufficient_ to
        !! evaluate either side of the equation to the end, and,
        !! moreover,
        !! $(fg)'$
        !! and
        !! $f'g + g'f$
        !! are found to have the same signature,
        !! so the equation is dimensionally consistent.

|> Exercise
    handle=_hello_world_
    Use dimensional analysis to
    conjecture a formula for

    $$
    \left({1\over f}\right)'
    $$

    for $f : \rr \ra \rr$ differentiable. 
    (The “building blocks” that appear in the formula
    should be $f$, $f'$.)

    !! for a generic differentiable function
    !! $f : \rr \ra \rr$ via dimensional analysis.

    |> Solution

        The expression

        $$
        \left({1\over f}\right)'
        $$

        does not on its own impose any restrictions on
        the signature of $f$, so we can consider

        $$
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
        $$

        of generic signature. Then

        $$
        {1\over f} : [\tA] \ra \left[{1\over\tB}\right]
        $$

        as in the second half of >>table_1_2,
        and

        $$
        \left({1\over f}\right)' : [\tA] \ra \left[{1\over\tA\tB}\right]
        $$

        !! dividing the output units by the input units.
        !! which divides the output
        !! units by the input units.
        per the usual effect of differentiation on units.

        In order to achieve the signature 

        $$
        [\tA] \ra \left[{1\over\tA\tB}\right]
        $$

        using

        $$
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
        $$

        and

        $$
        f' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\over \tA}\right]
        $$

        as building blocks we can start with $f'$ and divide
        by $f^2$. 
        (In fact, this is the only combination that works, modulo
        multiplication by a dimensionless constant.)
        Specifically, since

        $$
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
        $$

        we have
        
        $$
        f^2 : [\tA] \ra [\tB^2]
        $$

        and

        $$
        {1\over f^2} : [\tA] \ra \left[{1\over\tB^2}\right]
        $$

        multiplying $f'$ by ${1\over f^2}$ gives us a function
        of signature

        $$
        !! {f'\over f^2} : 
        [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\over \,\tA^{\vphantom{2}}}\cdot{1\over\tB^2}\right]\,\, = \,\,[\tA] \ra \left[{1\over\tA\tB}\right]
        $$

        as desired, which makes

        |> Boxed
            $$
            !! f'\cdot {1\over f^2} = {f'\over f^2}
            !! \left({1\over f}\right)' = {f'\over f^2}
            {f'\over f^2}
            $$

        a plausible conjecture.

        However, we know from Exercise X that

        $$
        \left({1\over f}\right)'
        $$

        should have opposite sign to $f'$, whereas

        $$
        {f'\over f^2}
        $$

        does _not_ have opposite sign, since $f^2$ is
        nonnegative. (Nb: When $f = 0$ neither $1/f$ nor
        $(1/f)'$ 
        nor $f'/f^2$
        are defined, so those points are not our
        concern.)
        !! positive. (Or zero, in which case _neither_
        !! $(1/f)'$ nor $f'/f^2$ are defined, which is
        !! another “good sign” for our conjecture.) 
        !! (The
        !! only “bad sign” for our conjecture is being the
        !! sign itself, right now!)

        Since our conjecture is at least “consistently
        wrong”—by which we mean that our formula _always_ has the wrong sign—a plausible fix
        is just to negate the expression (and indeed

        __negation__

        is one of the things we can do to a function
        without changing its signature, per >>table_1_2), giving
        us 

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \left({1\over f}\right)' = -{f'\over f^2}
            $$

        as an “educated guess” for a formula for $({1\over f})'$.

        !! |> SolutionNote

        !!     This boxed equation is the correct formula, and is called the
        !!     _reciprocal rule_.

|> Exercise
    handle=_guess_reciprocal_name_
    The formula given at the end of the solution
    to Exercise >>_hello_world_ happens to be the true,
    “correct” formula for $({1\over f})'$, for a differentiable
    function $f$. What _name_ do
    we expect this formula to have, as it takes its place
    in the mathematical pantheon?

    |> Solution

        Just as

        $$
        (f + g)' = f' + g'
        $$
        $$
        (fg)' = f'g + fg'
        $$

        are known as the _sum rule_ and _product rule_,
        so is

        $$
        \left({1\over f}\right)' = -{f'\over f^2}
        $$

        also a “rule”, the _reciprocal rule_. (!)


|> Exercise
    handle=_biz_

    Let

    $$
    f^{-1}
    $$

    stand for the “inverse” of a function $f$
    for which such an inverse exists. 
    This means that $f$ has the kind of graph
    below, in which distinct $x$'s are mapped to
    distinct $y$'s:

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/e6_f_inverse_distinct.svg

    ...and which means that we can invert the flow
    of the function, treating $y$'s as inputs, and recovering a
    unique $x$ for each $y$ that is an output of $f$—this
    “inverse flow” function is what we write as “$f^{-1}$”:

    |> Pause

    (In another view...

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/e6_f_inverse_box_view.svg

    ...$f^{-1}$ is the box that undoes the work
    of $f$ and vice-versa, in janother view...

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    (f^{-1} \circ f\rt{0.05}) = (x \ra x)\\
    \up{1.6}(f \circ f^{-1}) = (x \ra x)
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    !! is the identity function [_identity function_ = $x \ra x$],
    !! where $\te{id} = x \ra x$ is the identity function,
    ...$f^{-1}$ is the function that composes with
    $f$ 
    to produce the _identity function_ $x \ra x$ and vice-versa,
    and in another view...

    |> Pause

    ...$f^{-1}$ is the function whose graph is the
    mirror image of $y = f(x)$ about the line $x = y$.)

    Use dimensional analysis to conjecture a formula for
    the derivative of the inverse, i.e.,
    a formula of the form

    $$
    (f^{-1})' = \ldots
    $$

    similar to the sum rule, product rule, etc.

    !! where the formula can use any and all of

    !! $$
    !! f,\,f',\,\te{and}\,\,f^{-1}
    !! $$
    !! !! $f$, $f'$ and $f^{-1}$ 
    !! as “ingredients” on its right-hand side.
    !! !! Does geometry support your conjecture?